Reference_Answer:
Consider a simple C program where a parent forks a child. If the child process completes its execution and calls `exit()`, but the parent process fails to execute a `wait()` or `waitpid()` system call, the child process enters a zombie state. For example:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main() {
    pid_t pid = fork();
    if (pid == 0) {
        // Child process
        printf("Child running, PID: %d\n", getpid());
        exit(0); // Child exits
    } else if (pid > 0) {
        // Parent process
        printf("Parent forked child, PID: %d. Parent PID: %d\n", pid, getpid());
        // Parent does NOT call wait(). If it did (e.g., wait(NULL);),
        // the child would not become a zombie.
        printf("Parent sleeping for 10s. Child will become a zombie.\n");
        sleep(10); // Parent sleeps, child becomes zombie
        printf("Parent exiting.\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```
When this code runs, the child finishes and becomes a zombie until the parent eventually exits.
